<article class="env-theorem" id="025B">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/025B">Theorem <span data-tag="025B">41.12.1</span>.</a> Let $f : A \to B$ be an étale homomorphism of local rings. Then there exist $f, g \in A[t]$ such that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$B' = A[t]_ g/(f)$ is standard étale – see (a) and (b) above, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$B$ is isomorphic to a localization of $B'$ at a prime. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Write $B = B'_{\mathfrak q}$ for some finite type $A$-algebra $B'$ (we can do this because $B$ is essentially of finite type over $A$). By Lemma <a href="/tag/039L" data-tag="039L">41.11.2</a> we see that $A \to B'$ is étale at $\mathfrak q$. Hence we may apply Algebra, Proposition <a href="/tag/00UE" data-tag="00UE">10.144.4</a> to see that a principal localization of $B'$ is standard étale. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>